From 5c1a5964693b8d9e57b9ff119cfb7408d25550e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lucas Santos <lsantos7654@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Apr 2026 23:40:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 21/25] Detect disconnect via Node http events on
 @hono/node-server
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Production runs on `@hono/node-server`'s Node http adaptor (see
`server.ts:286` `createAdaptorServer({...})`), not native
`Bun.serve`. The presence model patches (0017/0019) used
`stream.onAbort` for cleanup on the assumption — drawn from Hono's
own commentary — that the responseReadable.cancel path is the
canonical disconnect signal on Bun >= 1.2. That's true for native
`Bun.serve`. It is NOT true for `@hono/node-server` running on
Bun's Node http compat:

  - Heartbeat writes succeed silently into the kernel send buffer
    after the peer FIN; Bun never observes an error and never
    cancels the response stream.
  - `stream.onAbort` therefore never fires.
  - `c.req.raw.signal` is supposed to fire from the adaptor's
    `outgoing.on("close", ...)` handler (node-server
    `dist/index.mjs:568-586`), but that event isn't reliably
    raised on Bun's Node compat for long-lived chunked responses
    either.

Reproducer (production server, before this fix):

  curl -N -H "X-Client-ID: foo" $URL/event &
  curl -X POST $URL/event/focus -H "X-Client-ID: foo" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"sessionID":"ses_x"}'
  pkill curl
  sleep 25
  curl $URL/global/live-sessions  # ses_x is STILL there  ← bug

Fix: the Node IncomingMessage and ServerResponse are exposed to
the Hono handler via the bindings argument
(`fetchCallback(req, { incoming, outgoing })` in node-server
`dist/index.mjs:587`). Reach them through `c.env.incoming` /
`c.env.outgoing` and hook directly:

  incoming.once("close", stop)
  incoming.once("aborted", stop)
  outgoing.once("close", stop)

These socket-level events fire when the kernel tears down the
connection, regardless of whether the higher-level
ReadableStream cancel chain triggers. `stop()` is idempotent, so
multiple sources firing is safe — whichever arrives first wins.

Also keep `stream.onAbort` and `c.req.raw.signal` listeners as
extra coverage for any topology where they DO fire (in-process
`app.request()` tests, future runtime changes).

Verification: `event.real-server.test.ts` spawns the
`@hono/node-server` adaptor on a random port, drives it from a
separate `curl` subprocess, kills the curl, and asserts the
client entry is removed. Two cases:

  1. kill curl -> entry gone within 500ms.
  2. two curls, kill one, only that one drops.

Both fail against the prior code; both pass with this commit.
The companion `event.test.ts` (in-process via `app.request()`)
remains green — 8 total green.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
